Run AnySYS under real DOS, on QEMU, against test floppies.

    tools/qemu/run.sh                  every case
    tools/qemu/run.sh ms622-used       just the named case(s)
    EXE=/path/OTHER.EXE tools/qemu/run.sh     test a different build

Build AnySYS first (wmake -h -f makefile.wat). Each case prints one line
per check and PASS or FAIL; the script exits non-zero if any case failed.
A full run takes about a minute.

Do not use DOSBox for any of this. It accepts INT 26h writes to the boot
sector of an image-mounted drive without saving them, and AnySYS then
reports success on a disk it did not change.

Cases
-----
toshiba-fresh
    Blank 720K floppy, Toshiba MS-DOS 2.11 kernels with /OEM left on AUTO,
    Toshiba's own boot sector as /BOOTSRC. The result must match Toshiba's
    original disk: root entries 0 and 1 (name, start cluster, size), the
    BPB, and the boot code from its jump target (24h) on. It cannot be
    boot-tested - Toshiba's original does not boot on QEMU either.

toshiba-used
    The used floppy (below), Toshiba kernels. Every file must survive the
    clusters being cleared for TBIOS.SYS, which must start at cluster 2.

ms622-used
    The used floppy, MS-DOS 6.22 kernels and boot sector. Same checks, with
    a 40-cluster IO.SYS that cuts through directories and a fragmented
    file. Then the floppy is booted on its own and must reach DOS and list
    the relocated A:\SUB - the loader found IO.SYS at cluster 2.

sys-drive
    ANYSYS C: A: with C:\SRCT (the Toshiba kernels) as C:'s current
    directory. As with SYS, a bare drive means its root, so the 6.22
    kernels in C:\ must be installed, not Toshiba's. Same checks as
    ms622-used, without the boot.

sys-default
    ANYSYS A: with no source, again from C:\SRCT. As with SYS, the source
    must be the root of the boot drive: the log must show "Source: C:\"
    and the 6.22 kernels must be installed.

The used floppy
---------------
fatcheck.py builds it identically every run (fixed-seed data) with every
low cluster taken:

    2 HELLO.TXT   3 SUB   4-6 SUB\F1.BIN   7 SUB\INNER   8-10 F2.BIN
    11-14 + 19-24 C.BIN (fragmented around B.BIN)   15-18 B.BIN   25-54 BIG.BIN

So clearing room at cluster 2 has to move a file, a directory (its "."
and its child's ".."), a nested directory, and a chain that is cut part
way through.

What it needs
-------------
qemu-system-i386, mtools, dosfstools, python3, and two reference disks
from "HDD for AnyDOS" (override with REF622= and TOSHREF=):

    C: boot    MS-DOS 6.22 hard disk image - boots DOS and runs AnySYS;
               also supplies the 6.22 kernels and boot sector
    Toshiba    MS-DOS 2.11 T1100 Plus floppy - supplies TBIOS.SYS,
               TDOS.SYS and its boot sector

Nothing is kept in this directory but the scripts; kernels and templates
are extracted from the reference disks each run. The reference images are
only ever read - C: is a copy.

How a run works
---------------
The floppy under test is A:. AUTOEXEC.BAT on C: runs CTTY COM1 before
AnySYS, which moves the console - stdout and stderr - onto the serial
port, and QEMU writes that to out/<case>/serial.log. COMMAND.COM can only
redirect stdout, and AnySYS reports every failure on stderr, so without
CTTY a failed run leaves nothing behind. A seven-byte QUIT.COM then writes
to QEMU's isa-debug-exit port: QEMU exit 1 is that clean stop, 124 means
the timeout (TIMEOUT=, default 120 s) fired instead.

Artifacts stay in out/<case>/: floppy.img (inspect with mdir, mshowfat,
fsck.fat -n), serial.log, qemu.log, and for ms622-used boot/serial.log
from the boot test.

fsck.fat and old boot sectors
-----------------------------
fsck.fat reads a volume label at 2Bh whatever the boot sector is, and a
pre-DOS 4 boot sector keeps code there - Toshiba's untouched disk draws the
same complaint. fatcheck.py excuses exactly that note when the boot sector
has no 29h extended-BPB signature, and says so on the fsck line.
